1
00:00:00,610 --> 00:00:03,270
Let’s look at another line of Swift together.

2
00:00:03,930 --> 00:00:09,180
Three runners crossed the finish line in bib order 3, 1, 2.

3
00:00:09,820 --> 00:00:11,440
We also need a list in bib-number order.

4
00:00:12,115 --> 00:00:17,375
After the second line runs, can we still tell who finished first?

5
00:00:18,760 --> 00:00:22,990
In Swift 2.2, sort() returned a sorted array.

6
00:00:23,640 --> 00:00:26,020
Bib 1 comes first in the new list.

7
00:00:26,680 --> 00:00:30,020
Bib 3 is still first in the finish record.

8
00:00:30,690 --> 00:00:35,860
To reorder the finish record itself, you called sortInPlace().

9
00:00:37,230 --> 00:00:43,660
In January 2016, the Swift community opened a review of standard library names.

10
00:00:44,335 --> 00:00:52,005
The proposal would apply the new guidelines to familiar tools like arrays and set an example for other libraries.

11
00:00:52,660 --> 00:00:56,560
The proposed name for getting a sorted result while keeping the original was sorted().

12
00:00:57,625 --> 00:01:01,655
The short verb sort() would reorder the array itself.

13
00:01:03,050 --> 00:01:09,290
Reviewer Radek Pietruszewski thought it mattered which operation got the shorter name.

14
00:01:09,950 --> 00:01:15,930
He argued that making the nonmutating version easier to type would encourage people to choose it.

15
00:01:16,590 --> 00:01:22,430
On March 3, review manager Doug Gregor announced acceptance with modifications.

16
00:01:23,840 --> 00:01:28,450
Let’s return to our finish record in Swift 3, released that September.

17
00:01:29,135 --> 00:01:33,295
With sorted(), we can make the list without separately saving the finish order.

18
00:01:33,965 --> 00:01:38,205
Calling sort() changes the first bib in the original array from 3 to 1.

19
00:01:38,870 --> 00:01:45,390
The proposal aimed to let callers read the name and tell whether they get a sorted value or change the one they have.
